---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Method & attributions"
  description="Where Every Cinema London's data comes from, what we change about it, and how the figures on each page are defined."
  section="about"
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>Method &amp; attributions</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every Cinema London is built on data and services generously provided by others. This page
      credits them, says what we change, and defines the figures the rest of the site shows.
    </p>

    <section id="clusterflick">
      <h2>Screening data — Clusterflick</h2>
      <a href="https://clusterflick.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <img class="cf-logo" src="/img/clusterflick.svg" alt="Clusterflick" />
      </a>
      <p>
        Cinema listings and showtimes (performances, venues, formats and accessibility details) are
        compiled by <a href="https://clusterflick.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Clusterflick</a>,
        which aggregates what&rsquo;s on across London&rsquo;s cinemas and venues.
      </p>
      <p>
        This project uses the published combined dataset from
        <a href="https://github.com/clusterflick/data-combined" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/clusterflick/data-combined</a>,
        and Clusterflick&rsquo;s hourly listing checks, published a day at a time as
        <a href="https://github.com/clusterflick/data-analysed/releases" target="_blank" rel="noopener">releases on github.com/clusterflick/data-analysed</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        What each venue put on sale, and when, comes from comparing consecutive
        <a href="https://github.com/clusterflick/data-combined" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data-combined</a>
        releases to each other. The comparison itself is Clusterflick&rsquo;s own, vendored from
        <a href="https://github.com/clusterflick/scripts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/clusterflick/scripts</a>
        (MIT) so that what counts as a new performance &mdash; and what counts as a rescheduled one
        &mdash; is decided in one place rather than two.
      </p>
      <p>
        Screening data from <a href="https://clusterflick.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Clusterflick</a>
        is published under
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY 4.0</a>,
        per its <a href="https://clusterflick.com/data-licence/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data licence</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>What we&rsquo;ve changed</h3>
      <p>
        CC BY 4.0 asks that changes be stated, and this site makes plenty of them. Nothing here is a
        verbatim copy of a Clusterflick release.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/venues/">Venue pages</a> show each venue&rsquo;s showtimes from the latest release,
          regrouped by day and film, with performances that have already started removed. The
          release is compacted first &mdash; lookups extracted, film fields stored once per film,
          performances reduced to id-referencing records.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/london/">Screening history</a> is reconstructed rather than republished. A release
          lists only <em>future</em> performances, so the record of what actually screened is
          assembled by partitioning consecutive releases into windows and bucketing each window&rsquo;s
          performances by hour.
        </li>
        <li>
          What each venue <strong>published</strong> is derived by diffing consecutive releases, with
          performances added to a run already listed and performances on a newly listed title counted
          separately, and each diff credited to the London day its interval started.
        </li>
        <li>
          Each venue&rsquo;s <strong>listing checks</strong> are aggregated from the hourly log into
          per-day, per-venue rates, with scoring Clusterflick does not publish &mdash; deciding which
          outcomes count for or against a venue, and leaving checks inside a declared closure out of
          the rate entirely.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="disclaimer">
        The CC BY 4.0 grant covers the screening data &mdash; performances, venues, booking links,
        formats and accessibility. It does not cover the film metadata shown alongside it: posters,
        genres, certificates, runtimes and directors come from TMDB under separate terms, below.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="method">
      <h2>How the figures are defined</h2>
      <p>
        The short version of the rules the charts follow. The full reasoning, including the mistakes
        that led to each one, is in the
        <a href="https://github.com/alistairjcbrown/everycinema.london#readme" target="_blank" rel="noopener">project README</a>.
      </p>
      <h3>A day is a London day</h3>
      <p>
        Every date and hour on the site is Europe/London, across the BST/GMT change. A release
        comparison is credited to the day its interval <em>started</em>: releases land about twice a
        day, and the hourly checks put most publishing in the evening side of an overnight interval.
      </p>
      <h3>Screenings are counted, not inferred</h3>
      <p>
        What an opening &ldquo;costs&rdquo; the films already playing is arithmetic: screenings gained
        minus screenings given up is exactly the change in the day&rsquo;s total. The site names both
        sides of a changeover and does not say which film took which screen.
      </p>
      <h3>Publishing means more was listed</h3>
      <p>
        On a venue&rsquo;s listing checks, &ldquo;new listings&rdquo; means its own listing count went
        <em>up</em> since the previous hour &mdash; not merely changed, since at midnight every venue
        drops the day that just ended. Venues whose source reports no countable volume are left out of
        that rate rather than scored zero.
      </p>
      <h3>A declared closure is not downtime</h3>
      <p>
        Checks inside a closure Clusterflick has written down leave the &ldquo;answered&rdquo; rate
        rather than counting against it, and are shown in their own colour instead.
      </p>
      <h3>What isn&rsquo;t recorded yet</h3>
      <p>
        Screening history is kept per film, not per venue, so venue pages show what&rsquo;s coming up
        rather than what played. Per-venue history is planned.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="tmdb">
      <h2>Film metadata — The Movie Database (TMDB)</h2>
      <a href="https://www.themoviedb.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <img class="tmdb-logo" src="/img/tmdb.svg" alt="The Movie Database (TMDB)" />
      </a>
      <p>
        Film metadata &mdash; titles, posters, genres, classifications, runtimes and directors &mdash;
        is sourced from <a href="https://www.themoviedb.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Movie Database (TMDB)</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="disclaimer">This product uses the TMDB API but is not endorsed or certified by TMDB.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="ag-charts">
      <h2>Charts — AG Charts</h2>
      <p>
        The interactive charts on the <a href="/london/">London</a> page are
        <a href="https://www.ag-charts.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AG Charts</a>. The charts on
        venue pages and the home page are drawn at build time and need no charting library.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="muted">
      Source code:
      <a href="https://github.com/alistairjcbrown/everycinema.london" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/alistairjcbrown/everycinema.london</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .wrap { max-width: 720px; width: 100%; margin: 0 auto; padding: 24px 8px 64px; line-height: 1.6; }
  h1 { font-size: 26px; margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .lede { color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 32px; }
  section { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 28px 0; }
  section h2 { font-size: 18px; margin: 0 0 10px; }
  section p { margin: 0 0 12px; }
  section h3 { font-size: 14px; margin: 22px 0 6px; color: #d4d4d8; }
  section ul { margin: 0 0 12px; padding-left: 20px; }
  section li { margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .disclaimer {
    background: #202024; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 12px 16px; font-size: 14px; color: #c4c4cc;
  }
  .tmdb-logo { width: 200px; max-width: 60%; height: auto; display: block; margin: 4px 0 16px; }
  .cf-logo { width: 72px; height: 72px; border-radius: 12px; display: block; margin: 4px 0 16px; }
  .muted { color: var(--faint); font-size: 13px; }
</style>
